; A container that holds children of its own head, and a reference that
; reaches one of them from outside.
;
; `space` declares the positional slot-local `space`: same head, its own
; body, no `:lowering`. So a nested `(space …)` resolves to the local and
; is data the container owns, which is what lets a `:lowering` container
; hold its own head without both hooks firing in one layer.
;
; What this case pins is the *other* half. The cross-ref index
; canonicalises the head string and never consults the slot registry it
; carries for the registration decision, so a nested local instance is a
; cross-ref target under `space` exactly like a root one. Both halves are
; observable here: `title` resolves from a sibling root, and the second
; `mark` collides with the first.
;
; `:depth` is declared on the local only, so accepting it on a nested
; space and nowhere else is the proof that the head resolved local-first.
;
; The kind targets `[space coords]` because a container that lowers is
; emitted under a second head — the list is the bucket key, so both heads
; share one namespace and a document resolves whichever of the two its
; host produced.
(plugin :name anm :version "1.0.0"
  (value-kind :name space-ref :underlying symbol
    :cross-ref (cross-ref :target [space coords]))

  (form :name space
    (key :name name :type symbol)
    (form :name space
      (key :name name :type symbol)
      (key :name depth :type number :optional true)))

  (form :name coords
    (key :name name :type symbol))

  (form :name text
    (key :name name :type symbol)
    (key :name space :type space-ref :optional true)))

(space :name poster
  (space :name title :depth 1)
  (space :name mark)
  (space :name mark))

(text :name letters :space title)
